A||||18||batboy||brake caliper won't compress||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||06-10-2000||10:56 AM||||I'm pissed at my 85 GT, continued...<P>Got the rear caliper on the driver's side loose from the rotor, after at least a 1/2 hour struggle and plenty of cussing (I'm beginning to hate this car). The pads were toasted and the rotor didn't look so good either. I have an extra good rotor, so no problem there. Looks like the e-brake cable is messed up, maybe causing this side of the rear brakes to drag at some point and to get hot. The previous owner warned me not to use the e-brake and I haven't since I owned it. Anyway, I can't get the caliper to compress with my C-clamp. Am I doing something wrong or is it stuck. I do have extra calipers, but I was hoping to avoid replacing it and the hassle of finding a neighbor willing to help me bleed the sucker. It's all disassembled out in my driveway right now with tools scattered everywhere. Please, I need help quick! Of course, Haynes is no help, it just says to compress it and install the new pads. Ok, but HOW?||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||06-10-2000||11:26 AM||||Never mind. I dug out one of my old good calipers and it compresses easily, so the other one must be stuck and is bad. At least I know now. I'm hot and mad and impatient and the bugs won't leave me alone and it's beginning to look like rain. What else can go wrong? The only good news is that my other pair of calipers has new looking semi-metallic pads on them, so I can take the other ones I just bought back for a refund. I'd much rather work on the Batmobile than this piece of guano. My GT fights me every step of the way.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sage||06-10-2000||11:29 AM||||The back pistons "turn in".  If you are reusing the pistons, it is best to get the tool used to turn GM pistons in, or just a good quality universal piston tool.  If you are replacing it, you can use a big pair of channel locks to turn it.  You have to get right on the lip and go slow.<P>Have used both methods, if you are careful, you CAN use the channel locks and not ruin the piston, but if you slip, you're kinda "done"<P>I think Autozone has a tool rental or borrowing policy if you are buying the parts to do the job at hand from them.  At least the ones around here do.<P>Good luck!  Oh yea, the pistons turn opposite directions on each side of the car.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">     Just my .002, hope it helps.<P>Just saw your second post..."nevermind"?!?!<p>[This message has been edited by Sage (edited 06-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||06-10-2000||11:48 AM||||I stand corrected. It is raining now. Damn lying weatherman said last night there was no chance of rain this weekend. $#!%, I hate Kansas weather! Time to take a cool shower and go into town to run errands and to get a nice lunch. At least the Batmobile is running ok again. I wish something would go right for a change. Anyone wanna buy a cheap 85 GT?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ed||06-10-2000||12:01 PM||||I'll never do my '87 rears again.<P>Last time, we had the proper tool, and we both had done rears before.  My helper was an experienced Air Force reserve mechanic.  But, the depressions in the pistons must have gotten rounded out over a couple of brake jobs, and it took HOURS.  The turning tool kept slipping out. You have to push and turn at the same time.  The engineers must have designed the system on the Friday they wanted to get out of town. <P>Hooking up the emergency brake cable is fun too. <P>Next rear brake job: new calipers.  I ain't doing it again.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||06-10-2000||04:00 PM||||Well, I'm back from running into town and I'm in a better mood. I'm just going to replace the caliper...it obviously got real hot from the e-brake dragging, so I'm not taking any chances.<P>While I was buying a 12 pack, the cute lady about my age at the counter asked "how's it going." I replied that I was in a pissy mood and needed to get laid or drunk, but since getting laid was out of the question, the 12 pack would have to do. She gave me a strange look and I regretting saying it while hoping she didn't slap me. Suddenly she burst out laughing and told me she wished that she could help me on both accounts, but they had a few laws against the other. That made me feel better.<P>Then I had a great lunch which made me feel even better yet. With a full tummy and cold Buds for later, I drove home. A car load of young ladies passed me and two of them waved at me...WOW! Ego was re-inflated and now I'm ready to take on that GT again...hehehe!<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||06-10-2000||05:33 PM||||Update: I changed out the caliper and was playing around with it. That piston won't turn either way. It's "frozen" up tight.<P>Also, my keyboard and mouse are all greasy, from taking many breaks from working to sit in front of the computer and A/C. Man, it's hot and humid. What happened to spring?<P>Now I have a strut bolt that won't come loose. Rusted solid too, I suppose. I'm soaking it in penetrating oil right now while I'm on break.<P>Oh crap, it's starting to rain again...I had to run out and put my tools up again.<P>Got a question. The rear struts on this 85 GT does NOT have the cam for alignments. My Batmobile (85 SE) and my 87 SE both had them. Can these be retrofitted? Are they available somewhere? I suppose I'll have to finish removing the strut to find out if the spindle is slotted on that lower bolt hole. This is probably why the alignment shop can't seem to ever get it aligned correctly.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||06-10-2000||06:19 PM||||Good ol' penetrating oil comes through for me again. I got the strut off in between rain showers. Ok, ok. Must of had a brain fart. It's the strut housing that is slotted, not the spindle. I also looked up those rear alignment kits that have the adjustable cam and pivot bolt: Moog part# K6264.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||2birds||06-10-2000||07:38 PM||||I just did the struts on a friend's Z24, and I was surprised the OEM struts didn't have the slotted lower hole.  I never saw the OEM ones on mt Fiero, but the Monroe Gasmatics have the slot.  I think maybe all the OEM ones were that way.  If you have the slotted struts, you shouldn't need the camber kits to get the adjustment right.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||06-10-2000||09:24 PM||||Yes, the Monroe struts that were on the Batmobile before I installed the coil-overs do have the slots. Those are the ones I'm putting on the 85 GT. I've got one side completely done now (including the brakes) and I figured out that you don't really need the strut cam, but it sure makes it easier to adjust. I put it back on without buying the alignment cam kit, mainly because I'd have to order it. I sure am glad I saved those struts and all the brake parts from the Batmobile (I did the Grand Am brake conversion, so I have a complete set of extra calipers and rear rotors). Looks like the other side is just as bad. I guess with 160k miles, you have to expect things to wear out. Well, I'm finished for the night, time to pop a top and relax. Anyone need a pair of totally worthless rear struts (all the fluid has leaked out) or a pair of good stock coil springs?||||reg||1
Z||000010||phillip||06-10-2000||09:30 PM||||i don't know if it's the same problem i had, not following directions. i spent a few hours trying to push one of my rear caliper pistons in. finally figured out that i needed to take the rotating arm off the back of the caliper. the piston slid right in. the other side took about 1/2 hour. hope you're not quite as stupid but find just as simple a fix. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||06-10-2000||10:52 PM||||Even with that arm off and trying to rotate the piston (wouldn't turn), the original caliper was stuck solid. I replaced it with a good used one that I had laying around.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||06-10-2000||11:49 PM||||Bats, yup, sounds like a cooked caliper.<P>others,<P>You DO NOT rotate stock 84-87 Fiero rear pistons to retract them. <P>The correct procedure is to remove the e brake arm, cover the e brake stud with a socket and press in the piston with a clamp. The stud will rotate under the socket as the piston is pressed in. This is the procedure listed in Every Fiero service book I can find including GM's.<P>on a good caliper the pistons will drop right in. the whole thing takes about 2 minutes.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||06-11-2000||10:56 AM||||Well, I just got chased inside again by more rain. But, the good news is that I pulled the passenger side caliper off and it's fine. The piston compresses just like it was supposed to and the rotor is reusable too. The bad news is that the motor mount on that side is totally shot. That accounts for an occasional clunk I hear if I let the clutch out too fast.<P>Sorry if this "blow-by-blow commentary" is boring, but while I'm waiting out the rain sprinkles, I'm bored too. I'm hoping maybe someone else can learn from my experiences too. I've got the strut bolts marinating in a lovely penetrating oil sauce right now.<P>So far, knock on wood, this side's been a breeze compared to the other side. Maybe the GT finally realized I wasn't going to give up, no matter how mad it made me get. Dad always told me: if at first you don't succeed, get a bigger hammer!||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||06-11-2000||11:07 AM||||A few of batboy's essential tools for working on a Fiero...LOL. NOTE: the caliper is wired up, never let it hang by the brake hose. This posed pic is actually of the Batmobile.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/batttools.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||batboy||06-11-2000||01:09 PM||||Well, got the lower strut bolts out, thanks to a combination of a long breaker bar, a large hammer, and penetrating oil. A thunderstorm just rolled in, dark sky and wicked lightning. I tell you, I'm cursed this weekend. My house will be paid off in 2002, then I'm building a 24' X 36' heated and air conditioned shop with air compressor, impact tools, and a gas torch...if I can only wait and survive that long...please Fiero Deities, give me the strength! No more getting wet, or laying on a nasty gravel driveway, or getting snow blown up the crack of my butt, etc, etc.||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||06-11-2000||08:31 PM||||The ol' 85 GT is back on the road again for a total cost of about $15 for the new sway bar end links. No more brakes pulling and squealing. No more excessive bouncing over every tiny bump, no more jingle bells rattling coming from the loose sway bar. Besides the new parts mentioned above, I installed the following used parts that were still in excellent condition salvaged from the Batmobile when I did the brake and suspension upgrades to it: shocks, strut assemblies, one rear caliper, one rear rotor, and rear brake pads. Wow, what a difference all this made. I can't believe I was driving it the way it was. Of course, now I have to get it aligned again. I still have one slight clanging noise in front when I go over a bad bump (maybe the rack?) and a couple lower motor mounts that need replace. Oh yeah, and the stupid leaking exhaust manifold. The car and the weather combined to put up quite a fight over the last few days and raised my blood pressure several times, but I was not going to give up. I'm more bull-headed and stubborn then it realized. Thanks for the support and letting me vent via the Forum. Like Red Green says: we're all in this together...||||reg||1
Z||000017||Sage||06-11-2000||08:47 PM||||BB;<P>Didn't mean to give you bad information on the calipers, but I was just telling you what has worked for me.  I have put pads on 3 of our pre 87 cars, and that's how they worked if you don't take the ebrake loose.<P>My Haynes manual says the same thing; press em in!  But trying that to no avail, I went to see a reputable mechanic freind of mine and he lent me the tool and tells me they twist in.  I told him about the manual and he says"f*** the manual!  "Thats why they have the detents in the pistons to accept the tool!"<P>So I don't know what to tell you, only that I did not intend to pass on bad information. <P>I don't pretend to be an expert, I only share what has worked for me.<P>Just glad that you got your car back on the road and that it works the way it is supposed to, regardless of the means.<P>Happy motoring to ya!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||06-11-2000||10:13 PM||||Sage, actually you did help me. Even though I had the e-brake lever off and was not clamping against the stud (this will cause caliper damage if you do, hence the reason for using a socket), I tried turning the piston when you suggested it. Of course it wouldn't turn, so I finally figured out it was truly frozen up and was only good for a core exchange. At that point I was ready to try anything. Believe me, Ogre has corrected me plenty of times too, so don't sweat it. All is well and I'm smiling again.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 06-11-2000).]||||reg||1
